ht now as  a nation is inflation and we have to  come down on that scourge of inflation  and it was interesting to me that Nigel  Lawson that most successful of  chancellors under Margaret Thatcher  endorsed Rishi sunak's proposals  precisely because he's willing to take  the difficult decisions to control  inflation because if we don't control  inflation it makes everyone poorer and  when inflation is running at 13 in a few  short years people can see their savings  their pensions income their their income  from work halved it is a really  pernicious thing that level inflation we  have to take the tough action now to  deal with it but also to help people in  the short term through this difficult  period which is why you saw the sort of  measures that Rishi has already  announced quite but Gordon Brown calling  it a financial Time Bomb is is no uh  small thing is it uh and finally you  know given that we've got these two  candidates with such differing views and  a level of backing from both uh from  across the party and the members what  happens when one of them becomes leader  is the government going to be singing  from the same hymn sheet or are we going  to see more disarray and chaos  no I think both sides will come together  in the National interest and I think  actually Gordon Brown's comments rather  illustrate this we all remember what  Gordon Brown did when faced with a  situation like this the poultry 75p  increase in the state pension it's  exactly that sort of thing that will  come together to resist and I'm  confident that we have two good  candidates to lead our country but I'm  also clear that Rishi sunax proposals  are up to the scale of the challenge  that we face and we have to get real as  a as a Nation about the scale of the  challenges we face and the leadership  that's required through it we're gonna  have to leave it there Oliver Darden  thanks very much indeed thank you
 

